Practical 6 – Inter Process Communication (IPC) Using FIFO
1. Aim
To implement Inter Process Communication between Client and Server using FIFO (Named Pipe).
2. Code Explanation
This practical contains two programs:
1. Client Code
2. Server Code
Both communicate using two FIFO files: fifo6.txt and fifo7.txt.
A. Client Code
Step 1: Header Files
#include<stdio.h>
#include<stdlib.h>
#include<sys/types.h>
#include<sys/stat.h>
#include<unistd.h>
#include<fcntl.h>
#include<string.h>


- stdio.h – Input/output.
- stdlib.h – Exit functions.
- sys/stat.h – FIFO creation.
- unistd.h – Read, write and close.
- fcntl.h – File opening modes.
- string.h – String operations.
Step 2: Create FIFO Files
int code6 = mkfifo("fifo6.txt",0666);
int code7 = mkfifo("fifo7.txt",0666);


- mkfifo() – Creates named pipes.
- fifo6.txt – Server sends message to Client.
- fifo7.txt – Client sends result to Server.
- 0666 – Read and write permissions.
Step 3: Open FIFO Files
int fd = open("fifo6.txt", O_RDONLY);
int fd2 = open("fifo7.txt", O_WRONLY);


- O_RDONLY – Opens FIFO for reading.
- O_WRONLY – Opens FIFO for writing.
- fd – Reads message from server.
- fd2 – Sends result to server.
Step 4: Read Message
res = read(fd, &Len, 1);

read(fd, stringBuffer, Len);
stringBuffer[(int)Len] = 0;

printf("Client Received: %s\n", stringBuffer);


- read() – Receives data from FIFO.
- Len – Stores message length.
- stringBuffer – Stores received message.
- printf() – Displays message.
Step 5: Count Words and Lines
while(stringBuffer[j]!='\0')
{
    char ch = stringBuffer[j];

    if((ch==' ')||(ch=='\n'))
    {
        w++;

        if(ch=='\n')
            line++;
    }

    j++;
}


- while – Checks every character.
- j – Character position.
- w – Counts spaces and newline characters.
- line – Counts newline characters.
- '\0' – End of string.
Step 6: Prepare Result
sprintf(str1," No.of Words : %d:::", w);
sprintf(str2," No.of Charecters: %d:::",(j-1));
sprintf(str3," No.of Lines: %d",line);


- sprintf() – Stores formatted result in strings.
- w – Word count as calculated by the code.
- j-1 – Character count excluding the null terminator.
- line – Line count.
Step 7: Send Result to Server
write(fd2, &LC, 1);
write(fd2, strMessage, strlen(strMessage));


- write() – Sends data through FIFO.
- fd2 – FIFO writing descriptor.
- strMessage – Contains word, character and line counts.

B. Server Code
Step 1: Open FIFO Files
int fd = open("fifo6.txt", O_WRONLY);
int fd2 = open("fifo7.txt", O_RDONLY);


- fd – Sends message to Client.
- fd2 – Receives result from Client.
Step 2: Enter Message
fgets(strMessage, 100, stdin);


- Takes message from the user.
- Stores message in strMessage.
- Maximum input size is 99 characters plus null terminator.
Step 3: Calculate Length
char L = (char) strlen(strMessage);


- strlen() – Calculates string length.
- L – Stores message length.
Step 4: Send Message
write(fd, &L, 1);
write(fd, strMessage, strlen(strMessage));


Sends message length and message to Client using FIFO.

Step 5: Receive Client Result
res = read(fd2, &len2, 1);

read(fd2, stringBuffer, 5000);

printf("Server Received: %s\n", stringBuffer);


- read() – Receives Client's result.
- stringBuffer – Stores received information.
- printf() – Displays the result.

